course: french
lesson: 1
title: Silent letters: why French doesn't say everything it writes
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: French writes lots of letters that nobody says out loud, a bit like the silent k in English "knee" and the silent b in "lamb".
explain: Think of the end of a French word as a sleepy tail: s, t, d, x and z there usually stay asleep, so Paris sounds like "pa-REE" and petit like "puh-TEE".
explain: A few end letters are usually awake, and the word CaReFuL helps you remember them: c, r, f and l, as in sac, bonjour, neuf and avril.
explain: A sleeping letter can wake up when the next word starts with a vowel: les amis sounds like "lay-za-MEE". This is called liaison.
explain: The letter h is never breathed out in French, so when a puffing English kid says "HO-tel" in Nice, the French say "oh-TEL", as if the h were invisible.
fact: Most letters at the very end of a French word are not said: s, t, d, x and z usually stay silent, so Paris sounds like "pa-REE".
fact: petit means small and sounds like "puh-TEE"; the final t is silent and the first e is a soft "uh".
fact: The memory word CaReFuL: a final c, r, f or l is usually said, as in sac ("sack"), bonjour ("bon-ZHOOR"), neuf ("nuhf", nine) and avril ("a-VREEL", April).
fact: The ending -er on a doing word, and the ending -ez, both say "ay": parler (to speak) sounds like "par-LAY" and parlez like "par-LAY" too.
fact: et means "and" and sounds like "ay"; its t is always silent: un chat et un chien (a cat and a dog).
fact: The letter h is never breathed out in French: hôtel sounds like "oh-TEL", hôpital (hospital) like "o-pee-TAL" and homme (man) like "om".
fact: In French ou says "oo" and oi says "wa": Louis sounds like "LWEE", moi like "mwa" and un croissant like "uhn krwa-SAHN".
fact: deux means two and sounds like "duh" said with rounded lips; the x is silent.
fact: Vous parlez anglais ? means "Do you speak English?" and sounds like "voo par-LAY ahn-GLAY?"
fact: Liaison: a silent final letter wakes up when the next word starts with a vowel sound, so les amis (the friends) sounds like "lay-za-MEE", with the s said as a z.
fact: After ils or elles (they), the verb ending -ent is silent: ils parlent (they speak) sounds like "eel PARL".
fact: On words that are not doing words, -ent can be heard as a nasal "ahn": le vent (the wind) sounds like "luh VAHN".
fact: Slang in this lesson: "plonker" (UK) is a silly person; "our kid" (North of England) means my brother or sister; "ken" (Scotland) means "you know"; "sus" means odd or suspicious; "bruv" means mate.
exercise: How do you say Paris in French? | "pa-REE", with the s kept silent | "PARR-iss", rhyming with Harris | "pa-REESS", with a loud s at the end | "PAIR-is", exactly like in English
exercise: Liam reads petit, which means small. How does it sound? | "puh-TEE" | "PET-it", with both t's said | "pet-EET", with a strong t at the end | "PEE-tie"
exercise: Which four end letters does the word CaReFuL remind you are usually said? | c, r, f and l | c, e, f and u | s, t, d and x | a, e, u and l
exercise: Which word ends with a letter you DO say, following the CaReFuL rule? | sac | petit | Paris | deux
exercise: How do you say et, the French word for "and"? | "ay" | "ett", like the start of Ethan | "eh-tuh", with a little t at the end | "eet", rhyming with "feet"
exercise: How does a French person say hôtel? | "oh-TEL", with no h sound at all | "HO-tel", with a big puff of breath | "ho-TELL-uh", with an extra sound | "hot-EL", like a hot bell
exercise: How do the French say the name Louis? | "LWEE" | "LOO-iss", with the s said | "LOO-ee", in two slow parts | "LEW-iss", like in Glasgow
exercise: How does deux (two) sound? | "duh", with rounded lips and a silent x | "dukes" without the k | "dewks", with every letter said | "deeks", rhyming with "weeks"
exercise: What does Vous parlez anglais ? mean? | Do you speak English? | Are you from England? | Do you like English food? | Can you write in English?
exercise: How does parlez end when you say it? | With an "ay" sound, and the z is silent | With a buzzing "z" sound, like in zoo | With a "lez" sound, as written | With an "ee" sound, like in "bee"
exercise: Why does the teacher say a "z" in les amis? | The s of les wakes up as a z before a vowel | French teachers add a z to make words sound nicer | amis is really spelled with a z at the start | The s is always said as a z in every French word
exercise: How do you say ils parlent (they speak)? | "eel PARL", with -ent silent | "eel par-LENT", like the word "lent" | "ills par-LAHN" | "eel par-LAY"
exercise: How do the French say un croissant? | "uhn krwa-SAHN", with no t | "a croy-SANT", with a hard t | "un kwa-SONT", with the t said | "un CROSS-ant", like in English
exercise: In French, what sound do the letters oi make? | "wa", as in moi and trois | "oy", as in "boy" | "oh-ee", in two parts | "o", as in "pot"
exercise: Which French word starts with a vowel SOUND, even though its first letter is not a vowel? | homme | chat | petit | deux
exercise: Which French word ends in a sound like "ahn", even though it ends in -ent? | le vent (the wind) | ils parlent (they speak) | elles chantent (they sing) | ils mangent (they eat)
test: Jade whispers, "it's French, they don't say the s, you plonker." ("Plonker" means a silly person.) Is Jade right about Paris? | Yes, the final s in Paris is silent | No, every letter is said in French | Yes, but only on Mondays in France | No, the s is silent only in English
test: Oliver goes, "why's she spelling careful with capitals, our kid?" ("Our kid" means my brother.) What is CaReFuL for? | Remembering that final c, r, f, l are usually said | Remembering which letters need capitals in French | Showing that "careful" is the same word in French | Practising handwriting before the French test
test: Your mate reckons, "Every last letter in French is silent, so neuf is 'neu' and bonjour is 'bonjou'." What is wrong? | f and r are CaReFuL letters, so they are usually said | Nothing, that is exactly how the French say them | Only the last letter of names is silent in French | Those words drop their first letter, not their last
test: Grace in Texas goes, "y'all, where's the t at in et?" What's the answer? | It's silent: et always sounds like "ay" | It's said loud, as in "Ethan" | It's said only at the end of a sentence | The app is broken, et has no t at all
test: Noah's sister laughs, "Mum, he's doing a dragon voice!" when he says "HO-tel". What would a French person say? | "oh-TEL", leaving the h out | "HO-tel", it is the same in every language | "khoh-TEL", with a scrape in the throat | "hoh-TELL", with a soft puff
test: A cartoon says the French word homme (man) like "hom" with a big breath. How should it really sound? | "om", with no h breathed out | "home", like an English house | "HOM-may", with the e said | "hum", like a bee
test: Jake goes, "bruv, that's not even a language" when Mohammed reads "vows par-lez ang-lays". ("Bruv" means mate.) What did Mohammed get wrong? | He said final letters that should be silent | He forgot to put the question mark at the start | He said the words in the wrong order | He should have spoken in Spanish
test: Emily is cross: "Where's the z in les amis? There isn't one!" What would happen if the teacher kept the s silent? | It would sound like "lay a-MEE", which is not how French people say it | It would turn into a different word meaning "the enemies" | Nothing, the French never join words together | The word amis would lose its first letter too
test: Jayden says, "why's ils parlent not got the -ent bit, that's proper sus." ("Sus" means odd.) Which rule explains it? | After ils or elles, a verb's -ent is silent | French people are too lazy to say the ends | The -ent is only silent when you whisper | Only boys' names drop the -ent ending
test: Isla's gran orders "a wee croy-SANT, ken." ("Ken" means you know.) What should she say? | "uhn krwa-SAHN" | "uhn croy-SAHN" | "uhn krwa-SANT" | "uhn kwa-SAHNT"
test: Ava in Auckland thinks deux sounds like "dukes" with no k, "sweet as". Which mistake is she still making? | She is saying an s sound for the silent x | She is saying the d, which is silent | She should say "dwa", like trois | She is saying the word too slowly
test: ★★★ Using the end-letter rules, which list has a sound at the end of EVERY word? | sac, neuf, avril, bonjour | sac, petit, neuf, avril | Paris, neuf, avril, bonjour | et, sac, neuf, bonjour
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the treatment of final consonants in French? | The consonants s, t, d, x and z, when terminal, are customarily left unpronounced | Every terminal consonant is to be pronounced with particular force and clarity | Terminal consonants are pronounced solely in the names of persons and places | Terminal consonants are omitted in writing as well as in speech
exam: Identify the pronunciation of the conjunction et, rendered in English as "and". | "ay", the terminal t remaining wholly silent | "ett", the terminal t being distinctly articulated | "eet", the vowel being considerably lengthened | "eh-tuh", a supplementary vowel being appended
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the initial letter h in the word hôtel. | It is never aspirated, so the word commences upon its vowel | It is aspirated vigorously, after the English manner | It is pronounced as a scraping sound in the throat | It is pronounced solely when the word appears in capitals
exam: It is given that the article les precedes the noun amis. Determine the pronunciation of the phrase thus formed. | "lay-za-MEE", the s being sounded as z by liaison | "lay a-MEE", the s remaining entirely silent | "less a-MEES", both terminal letters being sounded | "lez-AM-iss", in the English manner
